  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways a spherical particle can self-propel because its surface creates or responds to chemical asymmetry. In all cases, a particle of radius \\(R\\) moves with velocity \\(v\\) as an uneven distribution of surrounding solute or reaction products develops.\n\nOne mechanism is shown with a Janus particle whose two hemispheres behave differently: one side consumes protons while the other produces them. This creates an electric field and flow around the particle, driving motion. A related mechanism shows many charged species concentrated unevenly near the surface, especially on one side, generating an interfacial flow that also pushes the particle forward.\n\nAnother panel illustrates propulsion from a localized interaction site on an otherwise spherical particle. A small reactive or active patch of size \\(a\\) creates a strong local concentration gradient of surrounding solute, again leading to directed motion. The final schematic suggests motion driven by asymmetric release or interaction with nearby smaller droplets or particles, with the largest droplet moving away as the local environment becomes unbalanced.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that self-propulsion arises from symmetry breaking in chemical reactions, ion distributions, or interfacial flows around colloidal-scale spheres.",
